AC19 propagates receiver constness through any non-const method's return value, including one returning a fresh value rather than a view into this: measured at 10 false positives across 7 plugins in the engine population


## Found by lane-enum while measuring B120, 2026-08-31

## The defect

AC19 treats a method's return value as a **view into the receiver**, so receiver
constness travels through it. That is right for a method returning a reference
or handle into `this`. It is **wrong** for a method returning a FRESH value, and
nothing in the model distinguishes the two.

## The cost, measured rather than asserted

lane-enum's variant-C sweep widened propagation to the external/engine
population and produced **10 false positives across 7 plugins on shipped,
compiling code**, every one the same root cause:

- `tm-cotd-hud/Color.as:356` — `EscapeRawToOpenPlanet(const string &in raw)`,
  `auto ret = raw.Replace(...)`, then `ret = ret.Replace(...)`. `Replace`
  returns a **new** string; the propagation const-poisons it and flags the
  reassignment.
- `tm-match-recorder/Main.as:162-163` — identical shape on
  `const string &in filename`.
- `tm-birds/Filter.as:65` — `term.Split(" ")` then `parts[i] = parts[i].ToLower()`.
- `tm-archivist/HttpServer.as:313`, `tm-ghosts-plus-plus/HttpServer.as:333`,
  `tm-bosslike`, `tm-editor-plus-plus/ItemBrowser.as:1989-1990` — same class.

This is `lookup_method_return`'s "External types: no const-qualifier metadata
yet" comment **priced**. It is now a number in the code rather than a caution.

## Status after B120: symmetric, not correct

B120 shipped variant A only — propagation where we already emit — and
deliberately excluded C for exactly the above. So both halves of the rule now
behave the SAME way, which closed the parity break B120 was about. But both
halves share this defect, and the workspace half has no more "returns a view"
metadata than the typedb does.

**It is latent, not live: 0 fleet sites.** It only lands on calls the game
already rejects, so no shipped plugin can exhibit it. That is the same
structural zero as B116 and B120 — read the exposure hazard entry before
concluding the instrument is broken.

**Settle this before anyone widens propagation again.** The next person to look
at C will re-derive these 10 from scratch.

## What a fix needs

A way to know whether a method returns a view into the receiver. Candidates, in
increasing order of honesty:

- A per-method annotation in the typedb, if the dumps carry anything usable —
  check before assuming; B018's finding was that they carry no deprecation
  metadata, and this may be the same shape.
- Return-type shape as a proxy: a returned `T@` or `T&` MAY alias, a returned
  value type cannot. That is sound in the safe direction (never propagates
  through a by-value return) and would have killed all 10 above, since `Replace`,
  `Split` and `ToLower` all return fresh values. Cheap, partial, defensible.
- A deny-list, which is the wrong answer and is recorded here only so nobody
  proposes it as if it were new.

## Test axes

- A method returning a view into `this` (`T@` into a member, `T&` to a field)
  against one returning a fresh value (`string::Replace`, `array::Split`) —
  the pair is the whole point and either alone proves nothing.
- Value return, handle return, reference return, array return.
- Workspace receiver and engine receiver for each, since the two halves reach
  their return type through different lookups (`file_classes` members vs the
  pooled table) and B120 pinned that divergence but did not remove it.
- The 10 sites above as a regression corpus: any fix must leave all 10 silent.